### EcommerceDemo — API HTTP Examples
###
### All requests go through the BFF on port 5000.
### To test a downstream service directly, swap the @bff variable for the
### service-specific variable (e.g. @products, @baskets) and adjust the path
### (the BFF adds /api/identity/ prefix for Identity; downstream services use /api/customer).
###
### Usage in VS Code: install the "REST Client" extension, then click "Send Request"
### above each request block.

### ───────────────────────────────────────────────
### Variables
### ───────────────────────────────────────────────

@bff = http://localhost:5000
@products = http://localhost:5001
@baskets = http://localhost:5002
@orders = http://localhost:5003
@notifications = http://localhost:5004
@identity = http://localhost:5005

### Seeded product IDs (available after Products service starts)
@productId1 = 11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111
@productId2 = 22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222
@productId3 = 33333333-3333-3333-3333-333333333333

### Use the fake customer ID from the Identity service
@customerId = cust-001

### ───────────────────────────────────────────────
### 1. Product Operations
### ───────────────────────────────────────────────

### 1.1 — Get all products (via BFF)
GET {{bff}}/api/products
Accept: application/json

### 1.2 — Get all products (direct to Products service)
GET {{products}}/api/products
Accept: application/json

### 1.3 — Get a product by ID (via BFF)
GET {{bff}}/api/products/{{productId1}}
Accept: application/json

### 1.4 — Create a new product (via BFF)
POST {{bff}}/api/products
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Webcam HD",
  "price": 59.99,
  "category": "Electronics"
}

### 1.5 — Update a product (via BFF)
### Replace {{productId1}} with the ID you want to update
PUT {{bff}}/api/products/{{productId1}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "id": "{{productId1}}",
  "name": "Wireless Mouse Pro",
  "price": 34.99,
  "category": "Electronics"
}

### 1.6 — Delete a product (via BFF)
### Replace {{productId3}} with the ID you want to delete
DELETE {{bff}}/api/products/{{productId3}}

### ───────────────────────────────────────────────
### 2. Basket Operations
### ───────────────────────────────────────────────

### 2.1 — Get a customer's basket (via BFF)
GET {{bff}}/api/baskets/{{customerId}}
Accept: application/json

### 2.2 — Add an item to the basket (via BFF)
POST {{bff}}/api/baskets/{{customerId}}/items
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "productId": "{{productId1}}",
  "productName": "Wireless Mouse",
  "unitPrice": 29.99,
  "quantity": 2
}

### 2.3 — Add a second item to the basket (via BFF)
POST {{bff}}/api/baskets/{{customerId}}/items
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "productId": "{{productId2}}",
  "productName": "Mechanical Keyboard",
  "unitPrice": 89.99,
  "quantity": 1
}

### 2.4 — Remove an item from the basket (via BFF)
### Replace {{productId2}} with the product ID to remove
DELETE {{bff}}/api/baskets/{{customerId}}/items/{{productId2}}
Accept: application/json

### ───────────────────────────────────────────────
### 3. Checkout (Saga Orchestration)
### ───────────────────────────────────────────────

### 3.1 — Checkout the basket via the BFF saga orchestrator
### This executes: Step 1 (basket checkout) → Step 2 (order creation) → Step 3 (complete)
### On success, returns the saga ID and the created order.
POST {{bff}}/api/baskets/{{customerId}}/checkout
Accept: application/json

### 3.2 — Inspect saga state by ID
### Replace {{sagaId}} with the SagaId returned from the checkout response above
@sagaId = 00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000
GET {{bff}}/api/sagas/{{sagaId}}
Accept: application/json

### ───────────────────────────────────────────────
### 4. Orders
### ───────────────────────────────────────────────

### 4.1 — Submit an order directly to the Orders service
POST {{orders}}/api/orders
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "customerId": "{{customerId}}",
  "items": [
    {
      "productId": "{{productId1}}",
      "productName": "Wireless Mouse",
      "unitPrice": 29.99,
      "quantity": 2
    },
    {
      "productId": "{{productId2}}",
      "productName": "Mechanical Keyboard",
      "unitPrice": 89.99,
      "quantity": 1
    }
  ]
}

### 4.2 — Get an order by ID (via BFF)
### Replace {{orderId}} with the order ID from the checkout or submit response
@orderId = 00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000
GET {{bff}}/api/orders/{{orderId}}
Accept: application/json

### 4.3 — Get all orders for a customer (via BFF)
GET {{bff}}/api/orders?customerId={{customerId}}
Accept: application/json

### 4.4 — Cancel an order (saga compensating action, direct to Orders service)
### Replace {{orderId}} with the order ID to cancel
DELETE {{orders}}/api/orders/{{orderId}}/cancel
Accept: application/json

### ───────────────────────────────────────────────
### 5. Event-Driven Behavior
### ───────────────────────────────────────────────

### 5.1 — Trigger a ProductChanged event by updating a product
### The Baskets service consumes ProductChanged and updates basket item names/prices.
### The Notifications service also consumes ProductChanged and stores it.
PUT {{bff}}/api/products/{{productId1}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "id": "{{productId1}}",
  "name": "Wireless Mouse Pro Max",
  "price": 39.99,
  "category": "Electronics"
}

### 5.2 — Check notifications for received events
### After triggering product changes, basket checkouts, or order submissions,
### check here to see the events that the Notifications service consumed from RabbitMQ.
GET {{notifications}}/api/notifications
Accept: application/json

### 5.3 — Trigger a ProductChanged event by creating a product
POST {{bff}}/api/products
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Gaming Headset",
  "price": 79.99,
  "category": "Electronics"
}

### 5.4 — Check notifications again to see the new ProductChanged event
GET {{notifications}}/api/notifications
Accept: application/json

### ───────────────────────────────────────────────
### 6. Saga Failure & Compensation
### ───────────────────────────────────────────────

### To test saga compensation:
###
### 1. Add items to the basket:
###      → Send request 2.2 (Add item to basket)
###
### 2. Stop the Orders container to simulate a failure:
###      docker compose stop orders
###
### 3. Trigger checkout — the saga will:
###      Step 1: Basket checkout succeeds (basket cleared, snapshot captured)
###      Step 2: Order creation fails (Orders is down)
###      Compensation: Basket items restored from snapshot
###      Result: 502 Bad Gateway with "Checkout saga failed" message
###
POST {{bff}}/api/baskets/{{customerId}}/checkout
Accept: application/json

### 4. Verify the basket was restored:
GET {{bff}}/api/baskets/{{customerId}}
Accept: application/json

### 5. Restart the Orders container:
###      docker compose start orders
###
### 6. (Optional) Inspect the failed saga state:
###      Replace {{sagaId}} with the SagaId from the failed checkout response
GET {{bff}}/api/sagas/{{sagaId}}
Accept: application/json

### ───────────────────────────────────────────────
### 7. Identity
### ───────────────────────────────────────────────

### 7.1 — Get the fake customer (via BFF)
GET {{bff}}/api/identity/customer
Accept: application/json

### 7.2 — Get the fake customer (direct to Identity service)
GET {{identity}}/api/customer
Accept: application/json

### ───────────────────────────────────────────────
### 8. Full Demo Sequence
### ───────────────────────────────────────────────

### Run these requests in order for a complete demo walkthrough:
###
### Step 1: Browse products
###      → Send request 1.1 (Get all products)
###
### Step 2: Get the fake customer
###      → Send request 7.1 (Get customer)
###
### Step 3: Add items to basket
###      → Send request 2.2 (Add Wireless Mouse)
###      → Send request 2.3 (Add Mechanical Keyboard)
###
### Step 4: View the basket
###      → Send request 2.1 (Get basket)
###
### Step 5: Checkout (saga orchestration)
###      → Send request 3.1 (Checkout)
###      → Note the SagaId and OrderId in the response
###
### Step 6: Verify the order was created
###      → Send request 4.2 (Get order by ID) with the OrderId from Step 5
###
### Step 7: Verify the basket is now empty
###      → Send request 2.1 (Get basket)
###
### Step 8: Check notifications for received events
###      → Send request 5.2 (Get notifications)
###      → You should see BasketCheckedOut and OrderSubmitted events
###
### Step 9: Trigger a product change
###      → Send request 5.1 (Update product)
###      → Send request 5.2 (Get notifications) to see the ProductChanged event
###
### Step 10: View the distributed trace
###      → Open http://localhost:16686 (Jaeger UI)
###      → Search for traces from the "bff" service
###      → Expand a trace to see spans across BFF → Baskets → Orders → RabbitMQ → Notifications
